/**
  @page guides-getting-started-installation Installation and setup
  @brief Configure the dependencies, install the library, and verify an application.

  @tableofcontents

  @m_footernavigation

  Installation has three stages: configure Rodin with the dependencies for the
  desired features, build and install the library, then compile a small
  application against the installed package. Begin with a local installation
  before enabling additional solver or parallel backends.

  @section guides-getting-started-installation-requirements System requirements

  @subsection guides-getting-started-installation-required Required dependencies

  Rodin requires the following dependencies to be installed on your system:

  - **CMake 3.16.0+** - Build system generator
  - **C++20 compatible compiler** - GCC 12+, Clang 14+, or AppleClang
  - **Boost 1.74+** - C++ libraries collection
  - **Eigen3** - Linear algebra library
  - **HDF5 with C support** - Mesh and field storage
  - **Git LFS** - Required when building or installing resources

  @subsection guides-getting-started-installation-optional Optional dependencies

  For additional functionality, you may want to install:

  - **OpenMP** (`libomp`) - For parallel execution
  - **SuiteSparse** (`libsuitesparse`) - For additional linear solvers
  - **METIS** (`libmetis`) - For graph partitioning
  - **MPI** - For distributed computing
  - **PETSc** - For PETSc algebra and solvers (enabled by default)
  - **MMG** - For mesh optimization (included as submodule)

  @section guides-getting-started-installation-packages Installing dependencies

  @subsection guides-getting-started-installation-ubuntu Ubuntu/Debian

  On Ubuntu or Debian-based systems, install the required dependencies with:

  @code{.bash}
  sudo apt-get update
  sudo apt-get install cmake libboost-all-dev libeigen3-dev libhdf5-dev libomp-dev
  sudo apt-get install git-lfs  # needed for resources
  @endcode

  For optional dependencies:

  @code{.bash}
  sudo apt-get install libsuitesparse-dev libmetis-dev libopenmpi-dev
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-installation-macos macOS

  On macOS, use Homebrew to install dependencies:

  @code{.bash}
  brew install cmake boost eigen hdf5 libomp
  brew install git-lfs  # needed for resources
  @endcode

  For optional dependencies:

  @code{.bash}
  brew install suite-sparse metis open-mpi
  @endcode

  @section guides-getting-started-installation-rodin Installing Rodin

  @subsection guides-getting-started-installation-clone Clone the repository

  First, clone the Rodin repository with all submodules:

  @code{.bash}
  git clone --recursive https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin.git
  cd rodin
  @endcode

  @note The `--recursive` flag is important as it clones third-party dependencies
  included as git submodules. If you forgot this flag, run:
  `git submodule update --init --recursive`

  All files under @c resources/ are stored with Git LFS. If you need those
  resources, hydrate them after cloning:

  @code{.bash}
  git lfs install
  git lfs pull
  @endcode

  Library-only builds, CI-style checks, and installation tests that do not need
  the full resource tree can skip LFS hydration by configuring with
  `-DRODIN_INSTALL_RESOURCES=OFF`.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-installation-build Build and install

  Rodin uses CMake as its build system. There are two common installation
  methods:

  @subsubsection guides-getting-started-installation-user User-local installation (recommended)

  Install Rodin to your home directory without requiring sudo:

  @code{.bash}
  cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="$HOME/.local" \
    -DRODIN_USE_PETSC=OFF -DRODIN_INSTALL_RESOURCES=OFF
  cmake --build build --parallel 4
  cmake --install build
  @endcode

  This starting configuration disables PETSc and resource installation. PETSc
  is enabled by default; omit the `RODIN_USE_PETSC=OFF` option only when PETSc
  is available. Resource-dependent examples still require hydrated resources.
  The @ref configuration "configuration reference" lists the feature switches.

  To let applications find this installation, add the prefix to the CMake
  search path in the shell profile (`~/.bashrc` or `~/.zshrc`):

  @code{.bash}
  export CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="$HOME/.local${CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH:+:$CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH}"
  @endcode

  @subsubsection guides-getting-started-installation-system System-wide installation

  Install Rodin system-wide (requires sudo):

  @code{.bash}
  cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr/local \
    -DRODIN_USE_PETSC=OFF -DRODIN_INSTALL_RESOURCES=OFF
  cmake --build build --parallel 4
  sudo cmake --install build
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-installation-verify Verify an installed application

  Use the complete program and CMake project in
  @ref guides-getting-started-first-steps "First steps with Rodin". Configure it
  in a separate application directory after installation. It creates a mesh
  and a finite element space and reports 64 vertices, 98 triangles, and
  64 scalar P1 degrees of freedom.

  This checks package discovery, compilation, linkage, and a small runtime
  operation. It does not replace the library's numerical test suite.

  @section guides-getting-started-installation-troubleshooting Troubleshooting

  @subsection guides-getting-started-installation-cmake-not-found CMake can't find Rodin

  If CMake reports that it cannot find the Rodin package, ensure that
  `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` includes your installation directory:

  @code{.bash}
  cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/path/to/rodin/installation
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-installation-linker-errors Linker errors

  Link applications through the imported `Rodin::` CMake targets. Include
  targets for every module used, such as `Rodin::IO` for XDMF export. Check
  that the installed library and the application use compatible compilers
  and dependency installations.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-installation-lfs-resources Git LFS resources

  If a mesh file begins with a Git LFS pointer instead of mesh data, hydrate
  the resources from the repository directory:

  @code{.bash}
  git lfs pull
  @endcode

  If you do not need examples or installed resources, configure with
  `-DRODIN_INSTALL_RESOURCES=OFF`.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-installation-compiler-errors Compiler errors

  Verify that you're using a C++20 compatible compiler:

  @code{.bash}
  g++ --version  # Should be 12.0 or higher
  clang++ --version  # Should be 14.0 or higher
  @endcode

  @section guides-getting-started-installation-next What's next?

  Continue with @ref guides-getting-started-first-steps to build the verification
  application, then @ref guides-getting-started-first-problem for a complete solve.

  */
